 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "d5WvkPuqCwAJ",
   "from": "Jiang, Yuchao",
   "author": "c93b61679b5dd671",
   "date": "2016-02-26T22:09:08Z",
   "subject": "RE: question on CODEX: tuning of optimal K?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\">\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\">Hi Mari,<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\">Thanks for your interest in CODEX. As you can see from the in silico spike-in studies in the CODEX paper, CODEX has low sensitivity for common CNVs and we are currently working on a beta-version CODEX to solve\r\n this issue. Nevertheless, you GAA mutation is far from the common range of CNVs to be missed by CODEX. Can you give me some more background on your samples and the known heterozygous deletions?<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n<p><u></u><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><span>1.<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">      \r\n</span></span></span><u></u><span style=\"color:#1f497d\">Are your samples tumor samples or heterogeneous samples? If so you need to use the ‘fraction’ mode and then filter you cnv calls based on the fractional copy number estimated. Do you have normal control?\r\n If yes, use normalize2 with index of normal samples instead of normalize.<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p><u></u><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><span>2.<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">      \r\n</span></span></span><u></u><span style=\"color:#1f497d\">How many exons does your heterozygous deletion span? Have you checked whether these exons get filtered out in the QC step? Have you looked at your normalization results? You can take the log ratio of\r\n Yhat[[optK]]/Y_qc and find the corresponding samples that you know harbor the deletions.<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p><u></u><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><span>3.<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">      \r\n</span></span></span><u></u><span style=\"color:#1f497d\">What range of K did you give CODEX to run? What is the optimal K decided by CODEX? You heterozygous deletions might be removed as a latent factor if you K is set too large/stringent.<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p><u></u><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><span>4.<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">      \r\n</span></span></span><u></u><span style=\"color:#1f497d\">Have you loaded you calling results into IGV for visualization? See the bottom of\r\n<a href=\"https://sites.google.com/a/cornell.edu/yuchaojiang/home/research/codex\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://sites.google.com/a/cornell.edu/yuchaojiang/home/research/codex&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793243993433000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1EpxWu3bYKTb9b3Il6EcU_\">\r\nhttps://sites.google.com/a/cornell.edu/yuchaojiang/home/research/codex</a>  on how to do so. For our CODEX paper, we didn’t use all 1092 samples from the 1000 Genomes Project but only selected &lt;100 HapMap samples who also have SNP arrays as validations.<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\">Let me know if you have further questions. If you want to help you further and your data doesn’t involve human subjects / violates HIPPA, you can send me your save.image() r file and I might be able to help you\r\n figure out why.<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\">I am cc’ing our mailing list in case other people have similar questions.<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\">Cheers,<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\">Yuchao<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n<div>\r\n<div style=\"border:none;border-top:solid #e1e1e1 1.0pt;padding:3.0pt 0in 0in 0in\">\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>From:</b> Mari Mori [mailto:<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mari...@duke.edu</a>] <br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b> Friday, February 26, 2016 2:49 PM<br>\r\n<b>To:</b> Jiang, Yuchao &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">yuc...@wharton.upenn.edu</a>&gt;<br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b> question on CODEX: tuning of optimal K?<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Dear Yuchao,<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">I am using CODEX to demonstrate its sensitivity in detecting exonic deletions from WES data, and am writing to see if you have any advice on improving its sensitivity/specificity.<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">I have 97 samples each with known compound heterozygous GAA mutations (molecularly confirmed patients with Pompe disease).<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">14 of them have exonic deletions in GAA (chromosome 17).<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">I am trying to demonstrate if CODEX is able to detect these.<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">The samples were sequenced in 2 batched and I ran CODEX for each batch of BAM files. For the first batch (36 samples), which contain 4 samples with exonic GAA deletions, CODEX did not detect any GAA deletion. It detected 679 del/dups in\r\n the entire chromosome 17. K max was 2.  For the second batch (61 samples), which contain 10 samples with  CODEX detected only one homozygous large deletion. It detected 1565 del/dups in the entire chromosome 17. I did not record K mas for this run.<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Do you think I should tweak the K max to improve the sensitivity and specificity?<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Also, in your publication, CODEX detected 91 del/dups in 1000 samples. This is strikingly low compared to my result in 97 samples. Did you adjust any parameter to get this result?<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Thanks for you help.<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Mari<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u><span style=\"margin-left:208px;margin-top:189px;width:43px;min-height:33px\"><img width=\"43\" height=\"33\" src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/codex2/attach/baafb90af9577/image003.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\"></span><u></u><img border=\"0\" width=\"595\" height=\"375\" src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/codex2/attach/baafb90af9577/image004.png?part=0.2&amp;view=1\" alt=\"cid:ima...@01D17079.EA9C7190\"><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Mari Mori<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Medical Biochemical Genetics Fellow<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Division of Medical Genetics<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Department of Pediatrics<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Duke University Medical Center<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Phone: <a href=\"tel:(919)%20613-8313\" value=\"+19196138313\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">919-613-8313</a><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n"
    ]
   ]
