  {
   "id": "Xu9JjwfnDAAJ",
   "from": "Jiang, Yuchao",
   "author": "c93b61679b5dd671",
   "date": "2016-03-01T22:40:45Z",
   "subject": "Re: question on CODEX: tuning of optimal K?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">\r\nHi Mari,\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>If the BIC (default by CODEX) chooses the optimal K at 2 I think you should keep it as it is. You can use Z = log(Y_qc/pmax(Yhat[[optK]],0.001)) to get the normalized Z score. Each row corresponds to an exon and each column corresponds to a sample.\r\n You know which sample (aka, which column) has the deletion and the location of the exon (ref_qc is the IRanges object for exonic targets, use start(ref_qc) to get start positions and end(ref_qc) to get ending positions). Therefore you can plot the corresponding\r\n z-scores for that sample around that region to see if the signals are there.</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>BTW, did you check whether these exons get filtered out during your QC? You can look at the qcmat.txt file. </div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>Also by saying loading IGV I meant loading CODEX’s calling results into IGV for visualization rather than the bam files themselves. <span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:11pt\">See\r\n the bottom of </span><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/a/cornell.edu/yuchaojiang/home/research/codex\" style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:11pt;color:purple\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://sites.google.com/a/cornell.edu/yuchaojiang/home/research/codex&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793243993447000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2REVOUIX-r1bJWE36rk1W7\">https://sites.google.com/a/cornell.edu/yuchaojiang/home/research/codex</a><span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:11pt\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:11pt\"> on\r\n how to do so</span> but if you have a hard time (I feel you don’t use R as much) don’t worry about this — this is trivial and is only for sanity check by looking at the genome-wide results.</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>If after looking the z-scores you find that the signals are attenuated, then try optK=1 instead of 2. We know that a larger K tends to remove signals for common CNVs (in your case the CNV frequency is 14/97).</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>Cheers,</div>\r\n<div>Yuchao</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n<div>\r\n<blockquote type=\"cite\">\r\n<div>On Feb 27, 2016, at 2:26 PM, Mari Mori &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mari...@duke.edu</a>&gt; wrote:</div>\r\n<br>\r\n<div>\r\n<div>\r\n<div style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Dear Yuchao,<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Since the data is from humans in the study, I need to be sensitive about sharing the data. Our institution may consider de-identified WES data PHI (gray area) if it can be traced back to an individual from the\r\n fingerprint.<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">I have a basic knowledge of manupilating data frame and list in R. If you can instruct me on how to obtain variables of interest, I will get them. Then we can discuss tweaking parameters.<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Do you think it is doable?<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Mari<u></u><u></u></span></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div><div style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div>\r\n<div style=\"border-style:solid none none;border-top-color:rgb(181,196,223);border-top-width:1pt;padding:3pt 0in 0in\">\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<b><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Tahoma,sans-serif\">From:</span></b><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Tahoma,sans-serif\"><span> </span>Mari Mori<span> </span><br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b><span> </span>Saturday, February 27, 2016 12:14 PM<br>\r\n<b>To:</b><span> </span>&#39;Jiang, Yuchao&#39;<br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b><span> </span>RE: question on CODEX: tuning of optimal K?<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<u></u> <u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Dear Yuchao,<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n</div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
