    [
     2,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div><div style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div>\r\n<div style=\"border-style:solid none none;border-top-color:rgb(181,196,223);border-top-width:1pt;padding:3pt 0in 0in\">\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<b><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Tahoma,sans-serif\">From:</span></b><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Tahoma,sans-serif\"><span> </span>Jiang, Yuchao [<a href style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mailto:yuc...@wharton.upenn.edu</a>]<span> </span><br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b><span> </span>Friday, February 26, 2016 5:09 PM<br>\r\n<b>To:</b><span> </span>Mari Mori<br>\r\n<b>Cc:</b><span> </span><a href style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">codex_...@googlegroups.com</a><br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b><span> </span>RE: question on CODEX: tuning of optimal K?<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<u></u> <u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Hi Mari,<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Thanks for your interest in CODEX. As you can see from the in silico spike-in studies in the CODEX paper, CODEX has low sensitivity for common CNVs and we are currently working on a beta-version CODEX to solve\r\n this issue. Nevertheless, you GAA mutation is far from the common range of CNVs to be missed by CODEX. Can you give me some more background on your samples and the known heterozygous deletions?<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt 0.5in;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">1.</span><span style=\"font-size:7pt;font-family:&#39;Times New Roman&#39;,serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\">      <span> </span></span><span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Are\r\n your samples tumor samples or heterogeneous samples? If so you need to use the ‘fraction’ mode and then filter you cnv calls based on the fractional copy number estimated. Do you have normal control? If yes, use normalize2 with index of normal samples instead\r\n of normalize.<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt 0.5in;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">2.</span><span style=\"font-size:7pt;font-family:&#39;Times New Roman&#39;,serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\">      <span> </span></span><span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">How\r\n many exons does your heterozygous deletion span? Have you checked whether these exons get filtered out in the QC step? Have you looked at your normalization results? You can take the log ratio of Yhat[[optK]]/Y_qc and find the corresponding samples that you\r\n know harbor the deletions.<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt 0.5in;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">3.</span><span style=\"font-size:7pt;font-family:&#39;Times New Roman&#39;,serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\">      <span> </span></span><span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">What\r\n range of K did you give CODEX to run? What is the optimal K decided by CODEX? You heterozygous deletions might be removed as a latent factor if you K is set too large/stringent.<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n</div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div><div style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\"><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt 0.5in;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">4.</span><span style=\"font-size:7pt;font-family:&#39;Times New Roman&#39;,serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\">      <span> </span></span><span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Have\r\n you loaded you calling results into IGV for visualization? See the bottom of<a href=\"https://sites.google.com/a/cornell.edu/yuchaojiang/home/research/codex\" style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://sites.google.com/a/cornell.edu/yuchaojiang/home/research/codex&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793243993447000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2REVOUIX-r1bJWE36rk1W7\">https://sites.google.com/a/cornell.edu/yuchaojiang/home/research/codex</a><span> </span> on\r\n how to do so. For our CODEX paper, we didn’t use all 1092 samples from the 1000 Genomes Project but only selected &lt;100 HapMap samples who also have SNP arrays as validations.<u></u><u></u></span></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div><div style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Let me know if you have further questions. If you want to help you further and your data doesn’t involve human subjects / violates HIPPA, you can send me your save.image() r file and I might be able to help you\r\n figure out why.<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">I am cc’ing our mailing list in case other people have similar questions.<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Cheers,<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Yuchao<u></u><u></u></span></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(31,73,125)\"> </span></div>\r\n<div>\r\n<div style=\"border-style:solid none none;border-top-color:rgb(225,225,225);border-top-width:1pt;padding:3pt 0in 0in\">\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<b>From:</b><span> </span>Mari Mori [<a href style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mailto:mari...@duke.edu</a>]<span> </span><br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b><span> </span>Friday, February 26, 2016 2:49 PM<br>\r\n<b>To:</b><span> </span>Jiang, Yuchao &lt;<a href style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">yuc...@wharton.upenn.edu</a>&gt;<br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b><span> </span>question on CODEX: tuning of optimal K?<u></u><u></u></div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<u></u> <u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\nDear Yuchao,<u></u><u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<u></u> <u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\nI am using CODEX to demonstrate its sensitivity in detecting exonic deletions from WES data, and am writing to see if you have any advice on improving its sensitivity/specificity.<u></u><u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<u></u> <u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\nI have 97 samples each with known compound heterozygous GAA mutations (molecularly confirmed patients with Pompe disease).<u></u><u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n14 of them have exonic deletions in GAA (chromosome 17).<u></u><u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<u></u> <u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\nI am trying to demonstrate if CODEX is able to detect these.<u></u><u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<u></u> <u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\nThe samples were sequenced in 2 batched and I ran CODEX for each batch of BAM files. For the first batch (36 samples), which contain 4 samples with exonic GAA deletions, CODEX did not detect any GAA deletion. It detected 679 del/dups in the entire chromosome\r\n 17. K max was 2.  For the second batch (61 samples), which contain 10 samples with  CODEX detected only one homozygous large deletion. It detected 1565 del/dups in the entire chromosome 17. I did not record K mas for this run.<u></u><u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<u></u> <u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\nDo you think I should tweak the K max to improve the sensitivity and specificity?<u></u><u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<u></u> <u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\nAlso, in your publication, CODEX detected 91 del/dups in 1000 samples. This is strikingly low compared to my result in 97 samples. Did you adjust any parameter to get this result?<u></u><u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<u></u> <u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\nThanks for you help.<u></u><u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\n<u></u> <u></u></div>\r\n<div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">\r\nMari<u></u><u></u></div>\r\n</div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
